1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:09,364
Welcome to Episode Three, subtopic seven point two: Accumulation, Manipulation and Distribution, or AMD.

2
00:00:09,784 --> 00:00:16,768
In Episode One we used these three words inside the Power of Three. Now we study the phases themselves.

3
00:00:17,188 --> 00:00:24,628
You will learn what each phase looks like, how traders read the transitions, and how AMD differs from Wyckoff.

4
00:00:26,448 --> 00:00:36,336
AMD describes three phases of price behaviour around a range: a contained range, a false move beyond a boundary, and a directional expansion.

5
00:00:36,756 --> 00:00:42,828
It is widely used in ICT and SMC education to read how ranges resolve.

6
00:00:43,248 --> 00:00:49,200
Traders study ranges because liquidity tends to rest around their obvious highs and lows.

7
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:56,436
That is the key difference from a simple breakout pattern. AMD expects the first break to be a false move.

8
00:00:56,856 --> 00:01:03,840
And because it is a conceptual framework, the market does not have to complete all three phases neatly, or at all.

9
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:11,508
How does AMD relate to PO3? PO3 anchors the sequence to the opening price of a period.

10
00:01:11,928 --> 00:01:17,376
AMD focuses on the behaviour within each phase, and on the transitions between them.

11
00:01:19,196 --> 00:01:28,412
Phase one, accumulation. After a decline, price enters consolidation. Directional progress slows, and price moves sideways.

12
00:01:28,832 --> 00:01:34,736
Repeated highs and lows define the range boundaries: the range high and the range low.

13
00:01:35,156 --> 00:01:42,932
Where two highs or two lows are genuinely equal, we mark equal highs and equal lows. Here they match to the tick.

14
00:01:43,352 --> 00:01:49,760
Potential liquidity sits above the range and below it: buy stops above, sell stops below.

15
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:56,468
Inside the range, price fluctuates internally. Those internal swings are not yet the main event.

16
00:01:56,888 --> 00:02:05,840
One caution. A range is an observation. Calling it institutional accumulation is an interpretation that a chart alone cannot prove.

17
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:13,756
Phase two, manipulation. In a bullish context, price breaks below the range boundary.

18
00:02:14,176 --> 00:02:20,248
It trades through the range low and the equal lows, sweeping the sell-side liquidity beneath them.

19
00:02:20,668 --> 00:02:25,804
Then it rejects and returns into the range. Traders call this a false breakout.

20
00:02:26,224 --> 00:02:37,144
Notice the first, shallow break. Smart money traders often call this kind of early, obvious liquidity inducement: it tempts breakout sellers before the deeper sweep.

21
00:02:37,564 --> 00:02:44,644
In a bearish context it is the mirror image. Price sweeps above the range high, then rejects back inside.

22
00:02:45,064 --> 00:02:55,816
One more time, because it matters: manipulation here is an ICT label for a false move. It is not proof of illegal or coordinated market manipulation.

23
00:02:57,636 --> 00:03:05,004
Phase three, distribution. It begins with strong directional candles: displacement away from the sweep.

24
00:03:05,424 --> 00:03:09,864
Displacement often leaves an imbalance behind, a fair value gap.

25
00:03:10,284 --> 00:03:17,340
Structure develops. First a market structure shift above the last lower high, then a break above the range.

26
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:22,824
Price moves toward external liquidity, here the old high that began the decline.

27
00:03:23,244 --> 00:03:26,556
It trades through that high. What happens next is open.

28
00:03:26,976 --> 00:03:33,288
The move may continue, or it may exhaust. Distribution does not guarantee continuation.

29
00:03:33,708 --> 00:03:41,196
And remember: AMD distribution means directional expansion. It is not the Wyckoff distribution schematic.

30
00:03:43,016 --> 00:03:48,080
The full bullish AMD model. Step one: price consolidates.

31
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:51,932
Step two: the range boundaries are established.

32
00:03:52,352 --> 00:03:57,320
Step three: sell-side liquidity is identified beneath the range low.

33
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:00,836
Step four: price sweeps below the range.

34
00:04:01,256 --> 00:04:04,544
Step five: price returns into the range.

35
00:04:04,964 --> 00:04:08,180
Step six: bullish displacement develops.

36
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:15,152
Step seven: a bullish structural shift may occur, as price closes above the last lower high.

37
00:04:15,572 --> 00:04:19,460
Step eight: price expands toward buy-side liquidity.

38
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:24,488
Labelled together, these are the three phases of a bullish AMD.

39
00:04:26,308 --> 00:04:31,060
And the bearish model. Step one: price consolidates after a rally.

40
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:34,912
Step two: the range boundaries are established.

41
00:04:35,332 --> 00:04:40,084
Step three: buy-side liquidity is identified above the range high.

42
00:04:40,504 --> 00:04:43,528
Step four: price sweeps above the range.

43
00:04:43,948 --> 00:04:47,236
Step five: price returns into the range.

44
00:04:47,656 --> 00:04:50,872
Step six: bearish displacement develops.

45
00:04:51,292 --> 00:04:54,940
Step seven: a bearish structural shift may occur.

46
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:59,584
Step eight: price expands toward sell-side liquidity below.

47
00:05:00,004 --> 00:05:04,900
Accumulation, manipulation, distribution, in the bearish direction.

48
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:21,216
Now a mandatory comparison. In ICT and SMC, AMD is a price-delivery framework. In Wyckoff, accumulation and distribution are broader market-cycle structures with defined phases.

49
00:05:21,636 --> 00:05:31,572
In AMD, manipulation is a general interpretation of a false move. Wyckoff names specific events, such as the Spring and the Upthrust.

50
00:05:31,992 --> 00:05:42,384
AMD focuses on liquidity and price delivery. Wyckoff focuses on supply and demand, cause and effect, and effort versus result, usually with volume.

51
00:05:42,804 --> 00:05:49,908
AMD is commonly applied intraday and by session. Wyckoff is often applied to broader market phases.

52
00:05:50,328 --> 00:05:58,152
And the word distribution means opposite things. In AMD it is the expansion phase. In Wyckoff, it is a top.

53
00:05:59,972 --> 00:06:08,324
The same price action can be read by both methods. An AMD reader sees a range, a sweep, and the start of distribution.

54
00:06:08,744 --> 00:06:12,872
The evidence comes from liquidity, structure and displacement.

55
00:06:13,292 --> 00:06:20,732
A Wyckoff reader may see a trading range, a possible Spring in Phase C, then a test and a sign of strength.

56
00:06:21,152 --> 00:06:24,944
Their evidence comes from volume and effort versus result.

57
00:06:25,364 --> 00:06:31,628
Both can be useful. But use one vocabulary at a time, and do not mix labels without saying so.

58
00:06:33,448 --> 00:06:38,440
Common mistakes. One: treating every range as accumulation.

59
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:42,364
Two: treating every sweep as manipulation.

60
00:06:42,784 --> 00:06:48,616
Three: assuming distribution must follow immediately. Sometimes price ranges again.

61
00:06:49,036 --> 00:06:52,372
Four: confusing AMD with Wyckoff.

62
00:06:52,792 --> 00:06:57,328
Five: treating manipulation as proven institutional intent.

63
00:06:57,748 --> 00:07:02,500
Six: ignoring the higher-timeframe structure that the range sits inside.

64
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:07,696
And seven: forcing a bullish or bearish scenario because you want one.

65
00:07:09,516 --> 00:07:20,700
To summarise. AMD is a framework for how ranges may resolve. Accumulation is an observable range, with equal highs or lows only if they are genuine.

66
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:29,280
Manipulation is a sweep followed by a return inside the range. Distribution is displacement, structure and expansion.

67
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:34,884
AMD is not Wyckoff. Each uses its own vocabulary and evidence.

68
00:07:35,304 --> 00:07:40,872
And always remember: these labels describe price behaviour, not anyone’s intent.

69
